## Method
M01. Grok's suite, run unchanged in the copy: `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python -B app/tests/test_entry.py`.
M02. My own probe script (evidence/colab05.03/probes.py) imports the suite's fixture builders from the copy, so it uses Grok's fixture shape and not mine. It refuses to run against a path containing lib-build. It hashes the copy's tree before and after (excluding app/scratch) and restores any victim bytes it changed.
M03. The status probe (evidence/colab05.03/status_probe.py) calls context.validate_context directly for each of the six assignment states, with execute False and True.
M04. No worker, model or bridge was run. The bridge lab was read only, for the integration question.

## Observations
O01. Suite: exit 0, pass 10, expected-refusal 41, skip 0, fail 0, no bytecode. The copy sits in a different directory from the shared workspace, so this is also a relocation pass.
O02. The original APP-01 shape, with a complete fixture tree planted in app/ (record, payload, card, sample book) and fixture.json carrying output README.md and allowed_root ".", stops with "fixture root is not a designated container". README stays 64a7953d…. Grok's test plants only fixture.json in app/; the complete tree gives the same refusal, because select_container runs before any fixture read (librarian.py:85-91).
O03. A sibling prefix (0504.app-entry/fixtures-x) and another page's fixtures folder (0502.workflow-start/fixtures) both stop: "fixture root is the live workspace". is_inside appends a separator before its prefix test (context.py:102-104).
O04. Successes: a case-varied root in app/scratch from a foreign cwd; an explicit --container equal to the designated one; an external container. Each inventory equals expected-inventory.json.
O05. Refusals: an explicit container naming the other designated container; a root outside the named external container; an external root that is a junction to app/; a named container that is a junction to the workspace; an output directory junction to app/ ("path escapes the root").
O06. Refusal before import: a payload rewritten to create a marker file when imported never ran at a refused root, with a broken stamp, or with a CLI hash differing from the fixture hash. No marker was created.
O07. P8: the same marker payload, stamped and carded, with its own hash in both fixture.json and --sha256, ran inside app/scratch (exit 0, marker created). Nothing pins the reviewed payload hash dd6a78b5…: both hashes come from the caller and the fixture. README.md:21 says "This slice runs the one reviewed fixture payload". That holds only for the command that names it.
O08. P9: the approved payload, unchanged, in a fixture under app/scratch. Before the run, output/inventory.json.tmp was planted as a hard link to the copy's app/README.md. The run exited 0 with result: pass, and the copy's README became 1b465c3517a5b4575a3ff5b2cdbeeb37220d83b262afb5f69864b292093e13c0. That is the same after-hash as Grok's original failure (05.02.md:41). Cause: the payload writes its temporary file with write_bytes beside the output (fixtures/02.inventory.py:94-95), which writes through an existing link. The context and runner checks resolve paths (context.py:94-104, 553-564; 02.run.003.py:260-264), and a hard link has no path to resolve. The copy's README was restored and the tree check passed.
O09. Predecessors: 02.run.001.py, 02.run.002.py and 0302.run/02.md equal the suite's pins in the copy. Config selects 003 by path and hash.
O10. Status: with runner_root set to the code root, execute=False passes for all six states, and execute=True passes only for released ("assignment is not a fresh execution release"). With runner_root omitted, every call stops "runner is missing", because runner_root defaults to the fixture root (context.py:383-384).

## Integration reading (05.03 #02)
R01. The design's container placeholder (DESIGN.md row 61, "<APP-01 container metadata>") now resolves to nothing. The repaired interface keeps no container file: the container is config or --container. The glue must not read or write one.
R02. The bridge lab's write_atomic uses the same temporary-name pattern (labs/worker-bridge-pass01/bridge/canon.py:50-59). Its store writes sit in a delivery folder that is created fresh for each delivery, so a pre-planted link there is not reachable. The worker's output_root is a different matter: a reused <container>/bridge/output could hold one.
R03. The inventory runner pin is a prerequisite of validate_context even for bridge commands: it checks config runner presence, hash and band uniqueness. That is coupling, not a contradiction.

## Dispositions
N01. P9 is the one material finding (AR-01). I did not fix it; the app is out of my scope in this batch.
N02. P8 is a claim-versus-fact finding (AR-02). It is not a boundary escape.
N03. Not tested: time-of-check-to-time-of-use races between the guards and the payload's write; 8.3 short names; network paths. All three are deferred production concerns.
